  "text": "مسأل: The area of a rectangle without the quadrilateral APD is 50% of the area of the rectangle. This implies the area of PCD and APD are 50% each. We need to have the triangles equal or the quadrilateral equal to 50% of the area of the rectangle.\n\nLet the length of x be x and y be 10.5.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem for triangle APBD:\n\nx^2 + 17.4^2 = y^2\n\nNow, let's find the equation for P with respect to x, which follows PA+PB+PC+PD>7+y+x. As we already have the formula for x as a function of y, we can set y between 10.5 to 14 as however less than 10.5 we would get PA+7>x and more than 14 y>x can be found out and we can calculate PA+PC.\n\nLet's isolate for x first because we want to find x and y in the need of triangle APD's height like x/y+14/10.5 only has one real value.\n\n152/7 = y^2/x^2\n\nSo,\n\nx/y + 14/10.5 = 7\n\nx = 7 * (y/10.5) – 14/10.5\n\nCancel out common factor between 152 and 7:\n\ny = 7 * x / 224 = 7 * (y/10.5) – 14/10.5\n\nSplit the Fraction into Two Terms:\n\n7 * x / 224 + 14 / 10.5 – 7 / 10.5 = 152 / 176\n\nWe know that Akars’ approach is incorrect due to an algebraic error. Your approach’s starting point is right though, but we need the algebraic version corrected.\n\nI hope this helps. Stay tuned for the final answer within \\boxed{.}",
